y isn’t because the gasoline is colder than ground temp—it’s because as you pump it and it splashes, the rapid evaporation on your skin or the nozzle pulls the temp down much further. ### Why his experience is still valid (and incredibly useful) Mechanics develop an intuitive feel for fluids that’s often more predictive than physics textbooks. The fact that gasoline *behaves* like a refrigerant is crucial for safety and troubleshooting. But calling it “naturally colder” is a shorthand for a very real phenomenon: it has an extraordinarily low latent heat of vaporization *and* it’s extremely volatile. That combination means that whenever it’s exposed to your skin, it *creates* a cold environment on the spot, more aggressively than almost any other common liquid. That’s a truth born from decades of hands-on work, and it’s not wrong in practice—it just has a different underlying mechanism than a pre-existing temperature difference. One simple test that might connect both views: If your uncle ever uses an infrared thermometer on a small puddle of gas spilled on a warm engine, he’ll see it drop to maybe 40–50°F while the metal around it is still 80°F. He’d correctly say, “Look, it’s colder!” And he’d be absolutely right. But it’s colder because it *cooled itself*, not because it started that way. That self-cooling is so rapid and reliable that for all practical purposes, a mechanic can treat it as a substance that’s always cold on contact. I completely respect that hard-won intuition—it’s the same reason we instinctively blow on hot soup; we’re leveraging evaporation without doing the math. Your uncle’s experience is a perfect, real-world observation of that power.
